# bcrypt password verification (the $2a$ revision), in Dawn.
#
# It is here because auth.dawn was the last caller of org.mindrot.jbcrypt, and
# bcrypt is arithmetic over words and nothing else: there was never a reason for
# a password check to need a Java class, only the habit of reaching for one. The
# whole algorithm is Blowfish with an expensive key schedule (Provos and Mazieres,
# "A Future-Adaptable Password Scheme", 1999), so what follows is that and the
# bcrypt string format around it.
#
# `checkpw` is the whole public surface the backend needs. `hash_with_salt` is
# public only so the tests (and anyone checking a cost) can produce a hash from
# a caller-chosen salt; nothing in the server mints password hashes, the admin
# row is seeded by a script. Randomness is deliberately not in this file.
#
# ---- how the state is held, and why it is shaped like this ----
#
# Dawn has no mutable array: a List is persistent, there is no `xs[i] = v`, and
# an update by slicing is O(n). Blowfish's key schedule is nothing but ~8.5M
# single-word writes at cost 12, so a literal port would copy a 1042-word table
# for every one of them. The way out is that the schedule never writes at random.
# Each pass rewrites the 1042 words (18 P words, then four 256-word S-boxes) in
# strictly increasing order, so a pass can build its result by appending, which
# is O(1) amortized on a List, while reading from the *previous* state for
# every word it has not reached yet. `rd` is that merge: index k below the
# count already built reads the new list, otherwise the old one. That is exactly
# what an in-place pass would see, because an in-place pass also reads its own
# earlier writes and its own not-yet-written words. All four tables live in one
# flat 1042-word list (P at 0, S0 at 18, S1 at 274, S2 at 530, S3 at 786).
#
# It is slower than jBCrypt by a constant factor (about 10x on the JVM, measured
# in the commit that introduced this file), and the cost parameter makes that
# factor visible: re-measure before raising a cost anywhere. The 16 rounds are
# written out inside `rewrite`, the hot loop, rather than called as `encipher`:
# the call returned a tuple per block and cost a third of the run. `encipher`
# stays for the 64 final blocks, where it is not hot.
#
# Dawn's `Int` is 64-bit and signed, so every 32-bit sum ends in `& MASK`. The
# shifts are `>>>`; words are masked before they are shifted.

use std/bytes
use std/list.{drop}

const MASK: Int = 0xFFFFFFFF

## The initial Blowfish state: the first 1042 32-bit words of the fractional part
## of pi (18 P words, then S0..S3). Generated from pi with a big-integer
## Machin formula and checked against the published first, last and
## table-boundary words in the tests below, not copied from another implementation.
